WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.855
Voice AI agents can now answer

00:00:02.763 --> 00:00:04.249
calls and take action:

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:05.526
look up an order,

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:06.641
change a payout,

00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:07.790
issue a refund.

00:00:07.964 --> 00:00:10.192
But callers can pressure or trick

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:11.610
an agent into doing what it

00:00:11.506 --> 00:00:12.689
should never do.

00:00:12.923 --> 00:00:14.861
The question isn't whether the agent

00:00:14.756 --> 00:00:17.263
sounds smart. It's what stands between

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.900
its words and your money.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.692
BeyondFlow is a voice support agent

00:00:21.589 --> 00:00:23.457
for a sandbox treasury team.

00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:25.942
ElevenLabs gives it a natural voice.

00:00:26.001 --> 00:00:28.195
ArmorIQ decides what it is allowed

00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:28.937
to do.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.079
Here's the flow.

00:00:32.173 --> 00:00:33.970
The caller talks to an ElevenLabs

00:00:33.878 --> 00:00:36.003
voice agent. When the agent needs

00:00:35.887 --> 00:00:37.826
to act, it calls our gateway.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.496
The gateway asks ArmorIQ to approve

00:00:40.381 --> 00:00:42.237
the action, and only then does

00:00:42.121 --> 00:00:44.454
the request reach our MCP server,

00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:45.814
which holds the tools.

00:00:45.906 --> 00:00:47.844
Allowed calls return an answer.

00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:49.713
Blocked calls stop.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.571
Risky calls are held for human

00:00:51.445 --> 00:00:52.279
approval.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.400
First, ElevenLabs. We built a conversational

00:01:02.308 --> 00:01:03.503
voice agent on it.

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:05.524
Its tools are webhooks that point

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.395
at our gateway,

00:01:06.361 --> 00:01:08.508
so every spoken request becomes a

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.993
structured tool call.

00:01:10.168 --> 00:01:12.130
Because the webhook expects an answer

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:13.523
in about five seconds,

00:01:13.652 --> 00:01:15.671
our gateway replies quickly when a

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:16.472
call is held,

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:17.807
and the caller can ask for

00:01:17.704 --> 00:01:19.235
the supervisor's decision.

00:01:19.596 --> 00:01:22.415
ElevenLabs also generated the narration you're

00:01:22.301 --> 00:01:23.066
hearing.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.401
Now ArmorIQ. We registered our agent

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:30.456
and MCP servers on the ArmorIQ

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.882
platform and set three policies:

00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:35.669
allow reads, block payout changes,

00:01:35.646 --> 00:01:38.037
and hold refunds for two approvals.

00:01:38.165 --> 00:01:40.359
In code, our gateway uses the

00:01:40.289 --> 00:01:43.040
ArmorIQ SDK in three steps:

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.318
capture the plan,

00:01:44.341 --> 00:01:45.735
get an intent token,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.045
and invoke the tool through ArmorIQ's

00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:50.111
proxy. For held refunds,

00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:52.271
we open a delegation request and

00:01:52.201 --> 00:01:54.558
release the call once two supervisors

00:01:54.454 --> 00:01:57.194
approve. No allow or block decision

00:01:57.125 --> 00:01:58.354
lives in our code.

00:01:58.528 --> 00:02:01.129
A separate test shows ArmorIQ refusing

00:02:01.026 --> 00:02:02.395
a token used on the wrong

00:02:02.313 --> 00:02:03.045
tool.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.484
Now the boundary test,

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:09.284
recorded from a real run.

00:02:09.413 --> 00:02:11.572
'Where is my order?' Allowed.

00:02:11.699 --> 00:02:13.174
Asking the agent to send a

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:14.462
payout to a new account?

00:02:14.486 --> 00:02:15.820
Blocked by policy.

00:02:15.822 --> 00:02:17.213
A refund request?

00:02:17.192 --> 00:02:19.432
Held until two supervisors approve,

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:21.580
then released. Same agent,

00:02:21.545 --> 00:02:23.646
same voice, three outcomes,

00:02:23.611 --> 00:02:25.887
each decided by ArmorIQ.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.437
Typical safeguards check what is said,

00:02:35.531 --> 00:02:37.272
grant the same permissions for every

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.790
task, log what happened afterwards,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.567
or ask a human to approve

00:02:41.451 --> 00:02:44.679
everything. Intent enforcement checks the action

00:02:44.586 --> 00:02:46.443
itself, before it runs,

00:02:46.502 --> 00:02:48.568
against a plan and a policy,

00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:50.530
and involves people only when it

00:02:50.437 --> 00:02:51.354
matters.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.099
It's a prototype on sandbox data,

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:57.063
and one layer among several.

00:02:57.122 --> 00:03:00.998
BeyondFlow, powered by ElevenLabs and ArmorIQ.
